cut:  DescriptionSyntax Summary:  cut -c char_list [file_name ...]                 cut -f field_list [-d char] [-s] [file_name ...]         where:  char_list  is a list of integers that identify a character.                 field_list  is a list of integers that identify a field.                 file_name  is the name of a file.                 char  is a separator character.Description:     cut displays one or more specified columns or fields of a file on the     standard output.  Together with the paste command, it can be used to     reformat data files.  cut works by identifying characters or fields.     A field is composed of characters separated from the rest of the line by a     separator character.  A tab character is the default field separator.      cut counts tabs as one character, even though the tab might be equal     to 8 spaces. You can display certain fields or characters by specifying a     <field_list> or <char_list> on the command line.  If you don't specify a     file name on the command line, cut reads from the standard input. See     also: grep(1) and paste(1) for commands that might be used with cut.cut:  Options     -c char_list      <char_list> specifies a list of character position                       numbers to cut on.  Each number is separated by commas,                       and "-" indicates ranges.     -f field_list     <field_list> specifies a list of field position numbers                       to cut on.  Each number is separated by commas, and "-"                       indicates ranges.     -d char   sets field separator to <char>. (A tab character is the               default).  Characters with special meaning to the shell, must be               quoted.     -s   suppress the printing of lines that do not have field separators.cut:  Examples       cut -f3,5-7 datafile    (or:  cut -f3 datafile -f5-7)    Prints the third, fifth, sixth and seventh field of each line in the    file "datafile" in the current working directory. Tab is the default    field separator.       cut /dd/sys/password -f2 -d, ! qsort    Prints all passwords used in the system in an alphabetical order.    (superuser only)       printenv ! cut -f1 -d= ! qsort    Prints all environment-variables (names only) in an alphatetical    order.